Run the non-destructive restore test (uses throwaway containers only):
`sudo /usr/local/sbin/spark-backup-restore-test.sh [${MODELS_DIR}/backups/<snapshot>]`

## Snapshot contents

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `pg-litellm.dump` | `pg_dump -Fc` of LiteLLM DB `litellm` (online, consistent) |
| `pg-globals.sql` | `pg_dumpall --globals-only` (roles; the compose env recreates `litellm` anyway) |
| `webui.db` | Online `sqlite3 .backup` of Open WebUI's `webui.db` (integrity-checked) |
| `pocket-id.db` | Online `sqlite3 .backup` of Pocket-ID's `pocket-id.db` (integrity-checked) |
| `pocket-id-export.zip` | `pocket-id export` (official full export; usable with `pocket-id import`) |
| `vol-open-webui-data.tar.zst` | Volume `webui_open-webui-data` **minus** `./cache` (re-downloadable models) and the live `webui.db*` files |
| `vol-pocket-id-data.tar.zst` | Volume `webui_pocket-id-data` **minus** the live `pocket-id.db*` files |
| `config.tar.zst` | `/srv/*/` (compose, .env, keys, litellm.yaml, ...), `/etc/llama-swap`, llama-swap/docker-user-rules units, nvidia-persistenced drop-in, spark-backup and spark-offsite units, `/usr/local/sbin/*.sh`, `/etc/ufw/user*.rules`, `/etc/fstab`, `/home/${BACKEND_SSH_USER}/.hermes/config.yaml` (paths relative to `/`) |
| `manifest.txt` | Host, Docker/compose/Postgres versions, llama-swap version+sha256, every container's image ref and digest, volumes, file sizes |
| `SHA256SUMS` | Checksums. Verify with `cd <snap> && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS` |

The volume tarballs leave out the live SQLite files on purpose, because a copy taken while
the app is writing can be torn. **After untarring a volume, always copy the matching `.db`
file from the snapshot into it** (steps below).

The helper image `spark-backup-helper:alpine-5291449c` is alpine (pinned by digest) plus
sqlite and GNU tar. `spark-backup.sh` rebuilds it automatically if it is missing. You can
also use any alpine with `apk add sqlite tar`.

```bash
S=${MODELS_DIR}/backups/$(sudo awk '{print $2}' ${MODELS_DIR}/backups/LAST_OK)   # or pick a snapshot dir
cd "$S" && sudo sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
```

---

## 1. Restore single config files

```bash
sudo zstd -dc "$S/config.tar.zst" | sudo tar -tvf -                          # list
sudo zstd -dc "$S/config.tar.zst" | sudo tar -C /tmp/cfg-restore -xf - srv/gateway/litellm.yaml   # extract one file elsewhere
# or restore everything in place (ownership/modes preserved):
sudo zstd -dc "$S/config.tar.zst" | sudo tar -C / --numeric-owner -xpf -
```

## 2. LiteLLM Postgres (`gateway` project)

To restore into the existing DB, replacing its contents:

```bash
cd /srv/gateway
sudo docker compose stop litellm
sudo docker compose exec -T db pg_restore -U litellm -d litellm --clean --if-exists --no-owner --exit-on-error < "$S/pg-litellm.dump"
sudo docker compose start litellm
sudo docker compose exec -T db psql -U litellm -d litellm -c 'select count(*) from "LiteLLM_VerificationToken";'
```

To restore into a fresh, empty volume (rebuild):

```bash
cd /srv/gateway
sudo docker compose up -d db                # creates gateway_pgdata + litellm role/db from .env
until sudo docker compose exec -T db pg_isready -U litellm; do sleep 1; done
sudo docker compose exec -T db pg_restore -U litellm -d litellm --no-owner --exit-on-error < "$S/pg-litellm.dump"
sudo docker compose up -d
```

## 3. Open WebUI (`webui_open-webui-data`)

```bash
cd /srv/webui
sudo docker compose stop open-webui
# (optional) keep the current state: sudo docker run --rm -v webui_open-webui-data:/v:ro -v /root:/o alpine tar -C /v -czf /o/owui-before-restore.tgz .
sudo docker volume create webui_open-webui-data          # no-op if it exists
sudo zstd -dc "$S/vol-open-webui-data.tar.zst" | sudo docker run --rm -i -v webui_open-webui-data:/v spark-backup-helper:alpine-5291449c tar -C /v --numeric-owner -xpf -
sudo docker run --rm -v webui_open-webui-data:/v -v "$S":/s:ro spark-backup-helper:alpine-5291449c \
  sh -c 'rm -f /v/webui.db-wal /v/webui.db-shm && cp /s/webui.db /v/webui.db && sqlite3 /v/webui.db "PRAGMA integrity_check;"'
sudo docker compose up -d open-webui      # ./cache (embedding models etc.) is re-downloaded on first use
```

## 4. Pocket-ID (`webui_pocket-id-data`)

Pocket-ID files are owned by uid 1000. Its signing keys live in the DB, encrypted with
`ENCRYPTION_KEY` from `/srv/webui/pocket-id.env`, so **restore that env file from the same
snapshot**.

```bash
cd /srv/webui
sudo docker compose stop pocket-id
sudo docker volume create webui_pocket-id-data
sudo zstd -dc "$S/vol-pocket-id-data.tar.zst" | sudo docker run --rm -i -v webui_pocket-id-data:/v spark-backup-helper:alpine-5291449c tar -C /v --numeric-owner -xpf -
sudo docker run --rm -v webui_pocket-id-data:/v -v "$S":/s:ro spark-backup-helper:alpine-5291449c sh -euc '
  rm -f /v/pocket-id.db-wal /v/pocket-id.db-shm
  cp /s/pocket-id.db /v/pocket-id.db
  chown 1000 /v/pocket-id.db
  sqlite3 /v/pocket-id.db "PRAGMA integrity_check;"
  # The backup contains the old instance cluster heartbeat; with only one replica allowed a
  # fresh heartbeat blocks startup ("already one instance of Pocket ID running").
  # Clear it ONLY when no other Pocket-ID is running against this data:
  sqlite3 /v/pocket-id.db "DELETE FROM francis_hosts;"'
sudo docker compose up -d pocket-id
sudo docker exec pocket-id /app/pocket-id healthcheck
```

Alternative: run `pocket-id import` from `$S/pocket-id-export.zip` into an empty data volume
(see `docker exec pocket-id /app/pocket-id import --help`).

## 5. Host config: llama-swap, firewall, units

```bash
sudo zstd -dc "$S/config.tar.zst" | sudo tar -C / --numeric-owner -xpf - \
  etc/llama-swap etc/systemd/system/llama-swap.service etc/systemd/system/docker-user-rules.service \
  etc/systemd/system/nvidia-persistenced.service.d usr/local/sbin etc/ufw/user.rules etc/ufw/user6.rules
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart docker-user-rules llama-swap
sudo ufw reload
```

`/etc/llama-swap` is `root:llamaswap 750/640`. tar `-p --numeric-owner` keeps that only if
the `llamaswap` user/group IDs are the same (uid 995, gid 987). On a rebuilt host, check
with `chown -R root:llamaswap /etc/llama-swap`.

---

## 6. Whole-VM rebuild

1. **Base OS.** Install Ubuntu 24.04. Attach the `${MODELS_DIR}` disk (XFS) and add its fstab line
   from `config.tar.zst:etc/fstab`. Use `blkid` to confirm the UUID. Mount it: `sudo mount ${MODELS_DIR}`.
   Set up WireGuard `wg0` (${BACKEND_WG_IP}), which the units bind to. Its config is **not** in
   this backup; take it from the infra repo or a peer.
2. **NVIDIA.** Install the NVIDIA driver plus `nvidia-container-toolkit` (the manifest records
   the version), then run `sudo nvidia-ctk runtime configure --runtime=docker`.
3. **Docker.** Run `sudo apt install docker.io docker-compose-v2` (the manifest records the
   versions). Add `${BACKEND_SSH_USER}` to the `docker` group.
4. **Users.** Run
   `sudo groupadd -r -g 987 llamaswap && sudo useradd -r -u 995 -g 987 -G docker -d /var/lib/llama-swap -s /usr/sbin/nologin llamaswap`.
5. **Config files.** Pick `S`, run `sha256sum -c`, then restore all config in place:
   `sudo zstd -dc "$S/config.tar.zst" | sudo tar -C / --numeric-owner -xpf -`.
   This restores `/srv/gateway`, `/srv/webui` (and any other `/srv/*` stacks), `/etc/llama-swap`,
   units, scripts, ufw rules, fstab and the Hermes config.
6. **llama-swap binary.** It is not in the backup. Reinstall the version shown in the manifest
   (`llama-swap:` line, with sha256) to `/usr/local/bin/llama-swap`. GGUF models live on `${MODELS_DIR}`.
7. **Firewall.** Run `sudo ufw enable && sudo ufw reload` and
   `sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now docker-user-rules`.
8. **Gateway.** Follow section 2, "fresh volume": `docker compose up -d db`, then `pg_restore`,
   then `docker compose up -d`. Images are pinned by digest in compose.yaml and are pulled
   automatically.
9. **WebUI stack.** Run `cd /srv/webui && sudo docker compose create`. This creates the
   `webui_*` volumes and containers without starting them. Then do the volume steps in
   sections 3 and 4, including the `francis_hosts` cleanup, and run `sudo docker compose up -d`.
10. **Other `/srv/*` stacks** (for example monitoring): run `cd /srv/<x> && sudo docker compose up -d`.
    Only their config is backed up, not their data volumes.
11. **Host services.** Run
    `sudo systemctl enable --now nvidia-persistenced llama-swap spark-backup.timer`.
12. **Verify.** Check that the containers are healthy (`docker ps`), that LiteLLM accepts a key,
    that Open WebUI login through Pocket-ID works, and that `systemctl list-timers spark-backup*`
    shows the timer. Then run `sudo systemctl start spark-backup` and
    `sudo /usr/local/sbin/spark-backup-restore-test.sh`.

