## 7. Offsite copy (restic on S3)

`spark-offsite.timer` (04:30 UTC daily) copies all of `${MODELS_DIR}/backups` into the restic repo
`s3:s3.${RESTIC_REGION}.amazonaws.com/${RESTIC_BUCKET}/walter`. Retention: 14 daily, 8 weekly,
6 monthly. Each restic snapshot holds every local snapshot that existed at that time, so restoring one
gives you a `${MODELS_DIR}/backups` tree; then use sections 1-6 on the snapshot dir you pick.
Setup, key rotation and removal: `walter/backup/OFFSITE.md` in the repo.

To restore you need three things. Keep them together, offline:
1. **The restic password.** Its only copies are `/etc/spark-restic/password` on Walter and the owner's
   offline copy. Without it the offsite data is unrecoverable.
2. **An access key for the IAM user `spark-restic`.** If Walter is gone, create a new one with AWS admin
   credentials: `aws iam create-access-key --user-name spark-restic`.
3. **The bucket name and region** (`RESTIC_BUCKET`, `RESTIC_REGION` in site.env).

### 7a. On Walter (it still has /etc/spark-restic)

```bash
sudo -i
set -a; . /etc/spark-restic/aws.env; . /etc/spark-restic/restic.env; set +a
restic snapshots                                   # pick one, or use "latest"
install -d -m 700 ${MODELS_DIR}/restic-restore
restic restore latest --target ${MODELS_DIR}/restic-restore      # or: restic restore <id> ... --include /models/backups/<snap>
cd ${MODELS_DIR}/restic-restore${MODELS_DIR}/backups && for d in 20*/; do (cd "$d" && sha256sum --quiet -c SHA256SUMS && echo "ok $d"); done
S=${MODELS_DIR}/restic-restore${MODELS_DIR}/backups/<snapshot>   # then follow sections 1-6 with this $S
# afterwards: rm -rf ${MODELS_DIR}/restic-restore
```

### 7b. On a fresh machine (only the password and an AWS key)

```bash
# 1. restic, pinned and verified (same pin as walter/backup/offsite-setup.sh)
V=0.19.1; SHA=f415415624dcc452f2a02b8c33641791a8c6d6d3b65bbb3543fcf9a25151585c
curl -fsSLO https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/v$V/restic_${V}_linux_amd64.bz2
echo "$SHA  restic_${V}_linux_amd64.bz2" | sha256sum -c && bunzip2 restic_${V}_linux_amd64.bz2 \
  && sudo install -m 755 restic_${V}_linux_amd64 /usr/local/bin/restic
# 2. credentials: typed in, not stored in shell history or on disk
read -rp 'bucket: ' B; read -rp 'AWS access key id: ' AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID; read -rsp 'AWS secret: ' AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY; echo
read -rsp 'restic password: ' RESTIC_PASSWORD; echo
export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY RESTIC_PASSWORD AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=${RESTIC_REGION}
export RESTIC_REPOSITORY=s3:s3.$AWS_DEFAULT_REGION.amazonaws.com/$B/walter
# 3. restore (as root, into a root-only dir: the data contains every secret)
restic snapshots
sudo -E install -d -m 700 /root/restic-restore
sudo -E restic restore latest --target /root/restic-restore
sudo bash -c 'cd /root/restic-restore${MODELS_DIR}/backups && for d in 20*/; do (cd "$d" && sha256sum --quiet -c SHA256SUMS && echo "ok $d"); done'
unset AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY RESTIC_PASSWORD
```

Then rebuild with section 6, using `S=/root/restic-restore${MODELS_DIR}/backups/<snapshot>`
(or copy that snapshot dir into `${MODELS_DIR}/backups/` once the disk is mounted). Once the
new Walter runs, set up offsite again with `walter/backup/offsite-setup.sh`. To keep the same repo,
put the old password into `/etc/spark-restic/password` (root 0600) **before** running it; the script
then finds the repo and does not run `init`.

If objects were deleted from the bucket (for example by an attacker with root on Walter), the
noncurrent versions are kept for 30 days: see OFFSITE.md section 4.
